WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.982
Uncountable nouns.

00:00:02.022 --> 00:00:03.842
Why can’t we
add s to them?

00:00:03.882 --> 00:00:04.920
And why can’t
they take

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.384
a plural verb?

00:00:07.337 --> 00:00:09.267
Countable
nouns are easy:

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:10.948
a chair,
two chairs,

00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:12.225
three ideas.

00:00:12.265 --> 00:00:13.563
We count them
one by one,

00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:15.295
so they
can take s.

00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:17.192
But what
about water,

00:00:17.232 --> 00:00:19.529
rice, honesty,
or music?

00:00:19.569 --> 00:00:21.181
Nobody says
two waters,

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.791
or three
honesties.

00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:24.849
Most books
just give you

00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:26.323
a list
to memorise.

00:00:26.363 --> 00:00:28.717
We won’t.
We’ll use logic.

00:00:28.757 --> 00:00:30.632
Uncountable
nouns fall into

00:00:30.672 --> 00:00:31.972
five categories,

00:00:32.012 --> 00:00:34.735
and each category
has its own reason.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.836
One: unit,
or mass.

00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:38.968
Two: material.

00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:40.225
Three: measured.

00:00:40.265 --> 00:00:41.110
Four: abstract.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:41.603
And five:

00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:43.112
activity,
or process.

00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:46.515
Let’s prove them
one at a time.

00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:48.484
Error one.

00:00:48.524 --> 00:00:50.230
‘Furniture
are expensive.’

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.277
That’s wrong.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.738
The correct
sentence is:

00:00:52.778 --> 00:00:54.537
‘Furniture
is expensive.’

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:55.571
But why?

00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:57.438
Category one:

00:00:57.478 --> 00:00:59.298
unit, or mass.

00:00:59.338 --> 00:01:01.216
This kind of noun
is a unit,

00:01:01.256 --> 00:01:03.565
and a unit contains
many things.

00:01:04.203 --> 00:01:05.889
Look. A chair.

00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:07.459
A table. A bed.

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:08.783
A sofa. A desk.

00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:10.265
Many individual
things.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.862
English groups
them together.

00:01:12.902 --> 00:01:14.454
It draws one
circle around

00:01:14.494 --> 00:01:16.101
them,
and treats the whole

00:01:16.141 --> 00:01:17.283
group as one unit,

00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:18.765
called furniture.

00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:21.246
Why? Because
furniture

00:01:21.286 --> 00:01:23.316
is not the name
of one object.

00:01:23.356 --> 00:01:25.576
It’s the name
of the whole collection.

00:01:25.616 --> 00:01:27.457
The word comes
from ‘furnish’:

00:01:27.497 --> 00:01:29.725
everything you
furnish a room with.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.217
And the word
unit means one.

00:01:32.257 --> 00:01:33.299
One thing.

00:01:33.339 --> 00:01:35.236
And we can’t
add s to one.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.481
One is always
singular.

00:01:37.521 --> 00:01:39.039
We can’t add
s to one,

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:40.886
so the word
stays singular,

00:01:40.926 --> 00:01:42.604
and so does
the verb.

00:01:43.237 --> 00:01:45.196
So the correct
sentence is:

00:01:45.236 --> 00:01:47.403
‘The furniture
is expensive.’

00:01:48.537 --> 00:01:50.216
The old school
rule says:

00:01:50.256 --> 00:01:52.224
furniture,
luggage,

00:01:52.264 --> 00:01:53.099
baggage
and crockery

00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:54.285
are always
singular.

00:01:54.325 --> 00:01:55.368
Memorise it.

00:01:55.408 --> 00:01:57.827
Skip that.
Here’s the logic:

00:01:57.867 --> 00:01:59.169
many individual
things

00:01:59.209 --> 00:02:01.002
are named
as one unit.

00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:02.228
Unit means one,

00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:04.151
and one is always
singular.

00:02:04.903 --> 00:02:07.125
The same logic
works everywhere.

00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:07.921
Luggage:

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.713
bags and suitcases
together,

00:02:09.753 --> 00:02:11.466
one whole.
Clothing:

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:12.758
shirts
and trousers,

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:13.924
one category.

00:02:13.964 --> 00:02:14.540
Crockery:

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.845
plates and cups,
one collection.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.701
And if you
need a number,

00:02:18.741 --> 00:02:20.075
count a piece:

00:02:20.115 --> 00:02:21.405
a piece
of furniture,

00:02:21.445 --> 00:02:22.635
a piece
of luggage,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.396
an item
of clothing.

00:02:25.137 --> 00:02:26.406
Now think
about it.

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.152
Why can you say
‘two chairs’,

00:02:28.192 --> 00:02:29.687
but not ‘two
rice’?

00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:31.858
Error two.

00:02:31.898 --> 00:02:33.655
‘This ring
is made of golds.’

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:34.432
Wrong.

00:02:34.472 --> 00:02:36.499
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

00:02:37.237 --> 00:02:38.399
Category two:

00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:39.494
material.

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:41.028
A material
is what something

00:02:41.068 --> 00:02:42.172
is made from.

00:02:42.212 --> 00:02:43.659
It’s a substance.

00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:46.442
Take gold.
It’s a substance,

00:02:46.482 --> 00:02:47.726
and it’s used
to make many

00:02:47.766 --> 00:02:48.752
different things:

00:02:48.792 --> 00:02:51.301
a ring, a coin,
a crown.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:53.461
We can count
the things:

00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:55.468
one ring,
two coins.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.839
But the substance
itself

00:02:56.879 --> 00:02:58.928
is measured,
not counted.

00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:00.951
We weigh gold
in grams.

00:03:01.703 --> 00:03:03.215
So a material
noun,

00:03:03.255 --> 00:03:04.605
used
as a material,

00:03:04.645 --> 00:03:06.152
isn’t made plural.

00:03:06.192 --> 00:03:08.365
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

00:03:09.503 --> 00:03:11.300
The old school
rule says:

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.197
brick, sand, milk,

00:03:13.237 --> 00:03:15.198
glass and water
are always singular.

00:03:15.238 --> 00:03:16.314
Memorise it.

00:03:16.354 --> 00:03:18.447
Skip that.
The logic:

00:03:18.487 --> 00:03:20.134
it’s the material
used to make

00:03:20.174 --> 00:03:21.027
other things,

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.288
so you measure
it rather than

00:03:22.328 --> 00:03:23.461
count it.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:26.519
Silver:
used for jewellery,

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:28.077
measured,
not counted.

00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:29.194
Wood:
used for furniture,

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:31.156
measured
in cubic metres.

00:03:31.196 --> 00:03:32.912
Water:
used for drinking,

00:03:32.952 --> 00:03:34.585
measured
in litres.

00:03:35.237 --> 00:03:36.829
One careful note.

00:03:36.869 --> 00:03:37.888
When you
mean separate

00:03:37.928 --> 00:03:39.562
objects,
you can count them:

00:03:39.602 --> 00:03:41.001
a wall made
of bricks,

00:03:41.041 --> 00:03:43.073
or two glasses
of water.

00:03:43.113 --> 00:03:44.237
But as a material,

00:03:44.277 --> 00:03:45.546
it’s brick
and glass,

00:03:45.586 --> 00:03:46.819
with no s.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:48.935
Now think
about it.

00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:50.236
Do we say
‘one milk’,

00:03:50.276 --> 00:03:52.024
or ‘one litre
of milk’?

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.169
Error three.

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:56.192
‘I ate too many
rice for lunch.’

00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:56.876
Wrong.

00:03:56.916 --> 00:03:59.072
‘I ate too much
rice for lunch.’

00:03:59.803 --> 00:04:01.133
Category three:

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:02.104
measured.

00:04:02.144 --> 00:04:03.961
Food and substances
like rice

00:04:04.001 --> 00:04:05.618
are measured
by amount,

00:04:05.658 --> 00:04:08.073
not counted
as separate items.

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:11.324
Rice comes as many
tiny grains.

00:04:11.364 --> 00:04:13.339
We don’t count
them one by one.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:15.361
We measure
the amount instead:

00:04:15.401 --> 00:04:17.968
a cup of rice,
a kilo of rice,

00:04:18.008 --> 00:04:19.469
a plate of rice.

00:04:20.203 --> 00:04:22.487
Measured,
not counted,

00:04:22.527 --> 00:04:24.320
so it always takes
a singular verb.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.433
‘This rice
is delicious.’

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.280
Students often
confuse these

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.510
with material
or unit nouns.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:32.632
So just check:

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:35.196
is it measured,
not directly counted?

00:04:35.236 --> 00:04:37.544
The old school
rule says food,

00:04:37.584 --> 00:04:38.722
meat, sugar, salt,

00:04:38.762 --> 00:04:41.125
tea and bread
are always singular.

00:04:41.165 --> 00:04:42.900
The logic:
they’re measured,

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.120
not counted.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:45.446
Bread:

00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:47.441
measured in loaves
or slices.

00:04:47.481 --> 00:04:49.512
Milk:
measured in litres.

00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:51.700
Salt:
measured by weight.

00:04:52.437 --> 00:04:53.840
Now think
about it.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.377
Can you say
‘two rice’,

00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:56.617
or do you need
to say ‘two

00:04:56.657 --> 00:04:58.163
kilos of rice’?

00:04:59.503 --> 00:05:00.363
Error four.

00:05:00.403 --> 00:05:02.550
‘He has many
knowledges about art.’

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:03.417
Wrong.

00:05:03.457 --> 00:05:04.452
‘He has a lot
of knowledge

00:05:04.492 --> 00:05:05.661
about art.’

00:05:06.403 --> 00:05:07.560
Category four:

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.703
abstract.

00:05:08.743 --> 00:05:10.137
An abstract
noun has no

00:05:10.177 --> 00:05:11.435
physical form.

00:05:11.475 --> 00:05:13.425
It can’t be
held or touched.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.817
It’s an idea,
a quality,

00:05:15.857 --> 00:05:16.964
or a state.

00:05:17.004 --> 00:05:18.287
Knowledge,
honesty,

00:05:18.327 --> 00:05:19.829
advice, happiness.

00:05:19.869 --> 00:05:21.027
You can’t put
them on a table

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.388
and count them.

00:05:23.137 --> 00:05:24.795
You experience it,

00:05:24.835 --> 00:05:25.810
show it,
or possess it,

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.350
but you never
count it.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.583
So it’s singular:

00:05:28.623 --> 00:05:30.615
‘Her knowledge
is impressive.’

00:05:31.737 --> 00:05:33.185
The old school
rule says

00:05:33.225 --> 00:05:34.782
advice,
information,

00:05:34.822 --> 00:05:36.017
evidence
and knowledge

00:05:36.057 --> 00:05:37.554
are always
singular.

00:05:37.594 --> 00:05:39.625
Skip that.
The logic:

00:05:39.665 --> 00:05:41.291
we can’t physically
hold them,

00:05:41.331 --> 00:05:42.845
or count them
directly.

00:05:42.885 --> 00:05:43.898
Need a number?

00:05:43.938 --> 00:05:45.077
A piece of advice,

00:05:45.117 --> 00:05:46.503
a piece
of information,

00:05:46.543 --> 00:05:48.071
a piece
of evidence.

00:05:48.803 --> 00:05:50.185
Now think
about it.

00:05:50.225 --> 00:05:51.811
Can you hold
honesty in your

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.640
hand, or can you
only show it,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.945
have it,
or experience it?

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.274
Error five.

00:05:58.314 --> 00:06:00.417
‘She does many
works every day.’

00:06:00.457 --> 00:06:01.202
Wrong.

00:06:01.242 --> 00:06:03.404
‘She does a lot
of work every day.’

00:06:04.137 --> 00:06:05.509
Category five:

00:06:05.549 --> 00:06:07.747
activity,
or process.

00:06:07.787 --> 00:06:09.215
We talk about
an ongoing

00:06:09.255 --> 00:06:10.548
process
as a whole,

00:06:10.588 --> 00:06:12.311
not as separate
actions.

00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:14.907
Work has many
small steps:

00:06:14.947 --> 00:06:16.960
emails, calls,
meetings,

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.972
reports.

00:06:18.012 --> 00:06:19.356
But we talk
about it as one

00:06:19.396 --> 00:06:20.871
ongoing whole.

00:06:21.603 --> 00:06:22.634
One process,

00:06:22.674 --> 00:06:24.046
not separate
actions,

00:06:24.086 --> 00:06:25.682
so a singular
verb.

00:06:25.722 --> 00:06:27.689
‘Her research
is impressive.’

00:06:28.837 --> 00:06:30.687
The old school
rule says work,

00:06:30.727 --> 00:06:32.078
research,
progress,

00:06:32.118 --> 00:06:33.805
travel and exercise
are always

00:06:33.845 --> 00:06:35.874
singular.
Skip that.

00:06:35.914 --> 00:06:36.818
The logic:

00:06:36.858 --> 00:06:38.338
you’re talking
about the whole

00:06:38.378 --> 00:06:40.381
activity,
not separate actions.

00:06:40.421 --> 00:06:41.277
Research:

00:06:41.317 --> 00:06:43.202
the whole process
of studying.

00:06:43.242 --> 00:06:43.849
Travel:

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:45.651
going from
place to place.

00:06:45.691 --> 00:06:46.376
Shopping:

00:06:46.416 --> 00:06:48.795
the whole activity
of buying things.

00:06:49.537 --> 00:06:50.852
Now think
about it.

00:06:50.892 --> 00:06:52.315
Do we say
‘I do a work’,

00:06:52.355 --> 00:06:53.799
or ‘I do work’?

00:06:55.137 --> 00:06:56.532
Let’s put all
the examples

00:06:56.572 --> 00:06:58.175
on the board.

00:06:58.803 --> 00:07:00.129
Unit or mass:

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:01.584
furniture,
luggage,

00:07:01.624 --> 00:07:03.039
baggage,
equipment,

00:07:03.079 --> 00:07:04.494
machinery,
garbage,

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:05.604
rubbish, clothing,

00:07:05.644 --> 00:07:06.844
stationery,
crockery,

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:08.681
cutlery,
scenery and traffic.

00:07:08.721 --> 00:07:10.697
Many things,
one unit.

00:07:11.437 --> 00:07:13.731
Material: gold,
silver, iron,

00:07:13.771 --> 00:07:15.311
copper,
steel, wood,

00:07:15.351 --> 00:07:16.789
stone, sand, clay,

00:07:16.829 --> 00:07:18.318
cotton,
wool, silk,

00:07:18.358 --> 00:07:19.355
leather, plastic,

00:07:19.395 --> 00:07:20.328
glass and paper.

00:07:20.368 --> 00:07:22.083
It’s what things
are made of.

00:07:23.003 --> 00:07:24.832
Measured: food,
rice, bread,

00:07:24.872 --> 00:07:26.183
butter,
cheese, meat,

00:07:26.223 --> 00:07:27.450
sugar,
flour, salt,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:28.635
milk, water, tea,

00:07:28.675 --> 00:07:29.777
coffee and honey.

00:07:29.817 --> 00:07:31.345
Measured
by amount.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.870
Abstract: advice,
information,

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.131
knowledge,
education,

00:07:36.171 --> 00:07:37.219
honesty, justice,

00:07:37.259 --> 00:07:38.414
patience, courage,

00:07:38.454 --> 00:07:39.681
wisdom, happiness,

00:07:39.721 --> 00:07:41.727
freedom,
peace and evidence.

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:43.292
No physical form.

00:07:44.037 --> 00:07:45.781
Activity
or process:

00:07:45.821 --> 00:07:47.570
work, research,
progress,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.797
travel, exercise,

00:07:48.837 --> 00:07:49.973
training, sleep,

00:07:50.013 --> 00:07:51.568
shopping
and business.

00:07:51.608 --> 00:07:53.315
One whole
activity.

00:07:54.237 --> 00:07:56.223
So don’t memorise
the list.

00:07:56.263 --> 00:07:57.390
Find the category,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:58.875
and the logic
tells you:

00:07:58.915 --> 00:08:01.328
no s,
and a singular verb.

00:08:01.368 --> 00:08:03.611
That’s the Grammar
Logic Series.

